text:
Problem one: restoring a widget you have never heard of
To undo, you have to put an old value back into a widget. The obvious way is a lookup table:
sliderInput   -&gt; updateSliderInput
selectInput   -&gt; updateSelectInput
dateInput     -&gt; updateDateInput
# ... and so on, forever
That table is never finished. It breaks the first time somebody uses a widget from a package you have never seen. Every input package on CRAN would need an entry, and new ones appear all the time.
So rewind does not have one.
Every Shiny input registers an input binding against its DOM element. That is how Shiny itself talks to widgets. Each binding exposes setValue() or receiveMessage(). The client-side code looks the binding up and calls it:
var binding = $el.data("shiny-input-binding");

if (typeof binding.setValue === "function") {
  binding.setValue(el, value);
} else {
  binding.receiveMessage(el, { value: value });
}
That is the entire restore path. It works for third-party inputs for free, because it uses the same mechanism updateSliderInput() uses underneath.
The general lesson: when a framework already has a registry, use the registry. Do not build a parallel one you have to maintain.

Problem three: a bug that only R could give you
This one is my favourite, and it is the reason I think this post is worth writing for an R audience rather than a JavaScript one.
The capture observer looked like this:
shiny::observe({
  ctrl$note(ctrl$snapshot())
}, domain = session)
snapshot() reads every tracked input. Reading them inside observe() is what registers the reactive dependencies, so the observer re-runs whenever anything changes. Standard Shiny.
note() begins like this:
note = function(state) {
  if (private$.paused) return(invisible(FALSE))
  # ...
}
Now put those together with R’s lazy argument evaluation.
While capture is paused, note() returns at its first line. It never touches state. R evaluates arguments only when they are used - so ctrl$snapshot() never runs. And if snapshot() never runs, nothing reads the reactive values, so the observer registers no dependencies.
An observer with no dependencies is never invalidated. It never runs again. Not after rewind_resume(), not ever. A single rewind_pause() before the first flush silently killed undo for the rest of the session.
The fix is one line, and it looks like it does nothing:
shiny::observe({
  state &lt;- ctrl$snapshot()   # forces the reads
  ctrl$note(state)
}, domain = session)
Assigning to a local variable forces evaluation, the reads happen unconditionally, and the dependencies stay alive across pause and resume.
I find this one interesting because both halves are things R programmers know. Lazy evaluation is on page one. Reactive dependencies come from reading reactives during execution. It is the interaction that bites, and the symptom - “undo stops working, but only sometimes” - points nowhere near the cause.

Problem three: one row of a table
A table of revenue by region is a single pipeline that ends in group_by(region) |&gt; summarise(...). A viewer clicks the APAC row. What should the waterfall show?
The easy answer is to run the pipeline again for APAC alone. That answer is wrong as soon as a step after the summary compares groups:
sales |&gt;
  group_by(region) |&gt;
  summarise(total = sum(revenue)) |&gt;
  mutate(share = total / sum(total))
Run for APAC alone, share is always 100%.
So whence does not run the pipeline again for one group. It replays the steps of the whole table with a hidden id column, and reads the counts for the group from that. A step that compares groups keeps its true value. The code that it shows for a row is the code of the table, with one last filter() on the group.

A scene is a function of time
Each video has one HTML page, which I call a scene. The scene defines one function, window.renderAt(ms). It puts the page in the state that it has at that moment, and it does nothing else. It does not start a timer, and it does not wait for anything.
This is the key rule. If the page depends only on ms, then frame 120 is the same picture on every run. The recorder can ask for the frames in any order, as fast or as slow as it likes.
A scene describes its story as a timeline. Here is part of the rewind scene, a little simplified:
window.renderAt = function (ms) {
  // The drag runs from 1200 to 2200 ms. The region changes at 3650 ms.
  // Ctrl+Z at 4750 ms puts it back.
  const dragX = track(ms, [[1200, xOf(2024)], [2200, xOf(2020)]]);
  let year = ms &lt; 1200 ? 2024 : yearAt(dragX);
  let region = ms &gt;= 3650 ? "East" : "North";
  if (ms &gt;= 4750) region = "North";

  // The position in the history, as a list of steps.
  const index = stepAt(ms, [[0, 0], [2600, 1], [4100, 2], [4750, 1]]);
  document.querySelector(".rewind-undo").disabled = index === 0;

  setCaption(ms, [
    [900, 2900, "One drag is one step"],
    [2950, 4350, "Changing Region is another step"],
    [4450, 5700, "Ctrl+Z steps back: Region is North again"],
  ]);
  setVeil(ms, DURATION);
};
Every value on the page is computed from ms. Nothing remembers the last frame.

Small helpers for time
A few small functions do most of the work. They all take the time first.
// A value that goes from a to b between t0 and t1, with easing.
const tween = (ms, t0, t1, a, b) =&gt;
  lerp(a, b, ease(clamp((ms - t0) / (t1 - t0))));

// The value of a list of [time, value] keys at a time: eased between keys.
function track(ms, keys) {
  if (ms &lt;= keys[0][0]) return keys[0][1];
  for (let i = 1; i &lt; keys.length; i++) {
    if (ms &lt;= keys[i][0]) {
      return tween(ms, keys[i - 1][0], keys[i][0], keys[i - 1][1], keys[i][1]);
    }
  }
  return keys[keys.length - 1][1];
}

// The state of a list of steps, [time, value], at a time:
// the last step that has started.
function stepAt(ms, steps) {
  let value = steps[0][1];
  for (const [t, v] of steps) if (ms &gt;= t) value = v;
  return value;
}
track() is for things that glide: the pointer, a slider handle. stepAt() is for things that jump: a menu that is open or closed, the position in the undo history. With these two, a scene reads like a script for a film: at this time, this happens.
The parts that are not the app are drawn the same way. The pointer follows a list of [time, x, y] keys and makes a ripple at each click time. The caption is a pill that fades in and out between two times. The keys of a shortcut, such as Ctrl and Z, appear in the corner and press down at their time.
One more piece makes the video loop well. A white sheet covers the scene in the first 350 ms and fades in again in the last 450 ms. The last frame and the first frame are then both white, so the loop has no jump.

Things that move on their own
The rule “the page depends only on ms” is easy to break by accident. Three things can break it.
CSS transitions. A transition runs in real time, between two frames that the recorder takes. The shared stylesheet turns them all off:
*, *::before, *::after {
  transition: none !important;
}
CSS animations. The bones skeleton has a wave that moves across the grey bars. It is a CSS animation, and it belongs in the video. So the scene does not turn it off. It sets its time instead:
// Set the time of every running animation, and hold it there.
function freezeAnimations(ms) {
  for (const a of document.getAnimations()) {
    a.pause();
    a.currentTime = ms;
  }
}
document.getAnimations() returns every animation on the page. Each one is paused and moved to the time of the frame. The wave is then exactly where it should be at that moment.
This matters for the bones scene in another way. Its markup and its CSS classes are the ones that withBones() writes, and its stylesheet is the real bones.css from the package:
ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  withBones(plotOutput("chart")),
  withBones(tableOutput("results"), rows = 8)
)
So the skeleton in the video is drawn by the package, not copied by hand. Only the clock is new: renderAt(ms) sets the classes that the package’s JavaScript would set at that time.
Fonts. If the first frames are taken before the web font has loaded, they show a fallback font, and the text jumps a few frames in. The recorder waits for every weight of the font before it takes the first picture.

The recorder
The recorder is a Node script of about 100 lines. It uses Playwright to open the scene in Chromium, and ffmpeg to make the video.
The page is 640 by 360 pixels, at a device scale of 2. The pictures are then 1280 by 720, and the text is sharp. The script steps the clock 30 times a second and pipes each picture straight into ffmpeg:
const frames = Math.round((duration / 1000) * FPS);
for (let i = 0; i &lt; frames; i++) {
  await page.evaluate((ms) =&gt; window.renderAt(ms), (i * 1000) / FPS);
  const png = await page.screenshot({ type: "png" });
  if (!encoder.stdin.write(png)) {
    await new Promise((r) =&gt; encoder.stdin.once("drain", r));
  }
}
ffmpeg reads the PNGs from its input and writes an H.264 file:
ffmpeg -f image2pipe -framerate 30 -i - \
  -c:v libx264 -preset slow -crf 21 -tune animation \
  -pix_fmt yuv420p -movflags +faststart -an preview.mp4
-tune animation suits pictures with large flat areas, which is what a user interface is. -movflags +faststart puts the index at the front of the file, so the browser can start to play it before the whole file has arrived. -an means no sound.
Last, the script calls renderAt() once more, at a time that the scene chooses, and saves that picture as a JPEG. That is the poster: the image that shows before the video plays. Each scene picks a moment that explains itself. For rewind, it is the moment of Ctrl+Z, with both buttons in use.

The rail that scrolled the page
The history rail now scrolls only itself. Before this fix, an app with rewind_ui() in a sidebar could seem to scroll by itself each time the history changed.
I used this to keep the current step in view:
current.scrollIntoView({ block: "nearest" });
The name sounds like the right tool. But scrollIntoView() scrolls every scrollable ancestor, not only the nearest one. block: "nearest" limits how far each ancestor moves. It does not stop the sidebar or page moving.
The fix measures the step against the rail and sets the rail’s own scrollTop:
var top = current.getBoundingClientRect().top -
  rail.getBoundingClientRect().top + rail.scrollTop;
var bottom = top + current.offsetHeight;

if (top &lt; rail.scrollTop) {
  rail.scrollTop = top;
} else if (bottom &gt; rail.scrollTop + rail.clientHeight) {
  rail.scrollTop = bottom - rail.clientHeight;
}
The general lesson: when a control owns a small scrolling area, move that area directly. A browser method that promises to bring something into view may move more of the page than you expect.
